  "query_preview": "..\nToday and tomorrow: An upper level low offshore Oregon this \nafternoon will slowly move inland tonight, bringing widespread rains \nand a small chance (10-15%) of thunderstorms starting around 11 PM \nthis evening in southern Chelan and Grant County and spreading \nnortheast through the night hours. An occluded front will pass \nthrough the region in the late morning, leaving eastern WA and north \nID in a showery regime while north central WA stays in a stable \nprecipitation regime in the wraparound region. Weak surface \nbased instability (50-300 J/kg) rebuilds in Sunday afternoon in \neastern WA and north ID as day time heating occurs, bringing an \nadditional 15% chance of thunderstorms. There has been an \nundeniable trend towards less precipitation amounts in southeast\nWA and the southern ID Panhandle and more precipitation amounts\nin the Cascades due to the nature of showery precipitation \nversus stratiform. The upper low departs in northwest MT Sunday \nnight, bringing additional light wraparound rain to eastern WA \nand north ID overnight into Monday. The National Blend of Models\nhas struggled badly with this system, but the probabilities of \n1+ inch of rain through 5 AM Monday appear more trustworthy at \nthis point as there is more ingestion of high resolution models:\n\nWenatchee: 81%\nChelan: 57%\nOmak: 39% \nColville: 31% \nMoses Lake: 21% \nPullman: 16% \nSpokane: 14% \nLewiston: 13% \nSandpoint: 13%\n\nMonday through Saturday: Monday will be mostly dry as the trough \nshifts into the north central US. Temperatures will be cool in the \nupper 50s through low 70s. There will be some lingering showers in \nthe ID Panhandle in the early afternoon with upslope flow into the \nmountains. Another trough sweeps through southwestern British \nColumbia Tuesday, bringing another round of light showers to the \nnorthern mountains and Cascades. The remainder of next week is \ndominated by high pressure in the northern Pacific shifting eastward \ninto the Pacific NW. Temperatures will rebound into the 70s and 80s \nwith lots of sunshine. Will need to monitor for frost potential in \nthe sheltered northern valleys. /Butler",
